1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,478
Evening one took a whole lecture to build.

2
00:00:04,378 --> 00:00:08,291
Evening two is going to take a few minutes.

3
00:00:10,091 --> 00:00:14,004
Because today, you write down what stays the same.

4
00:00:14,904 --> 00:00:16,643
That's called a pattern,

5
00:00:17,093 --> 00:00:22,311
and a campaign is one pattern that you fill in twenty times.

6
00:00:23,511 --> 00:00:27,859
The people who built Storm's Beacon made themselves a rule.

7
00:00:28,759 --> 00:00:33,107
Adding an episode has to be a few mechanical edits,

8
00:00:33,557 --> 00:00:36,165
and no new thinking about how.

9
00:00:37,965 --> 00:00:39,704
And they tested it.

10
00:00:40,154 --> 00:00:44,937
They added a third episode to a campaign that had two.

11
00:00:45,837 --> 00:00:48,880
It held, so they wrote the rest.

12
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:53,724
You already have what an episode needs.

13
00:00:54,624 --> 00:00:57,667
A lead that ends at a place.

14
00:00:58,117 --> 00:01:01,161
A line that shows the next step.

15
00:01:02,061 --> 00:01:04,670
Guards and cover on a landmark.

16
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:09,033
And a step that ends at home and pays.

17
00:01:10,833 --> 00:01:13,007
None of that's new today.

18
00:01:13,907 --> 00:01:17,385
Today's about writing down what stays the same,

19
00:01:17,835 --> 00:01:21,748
so you never have to work it out again.

20
00:01:22,948 --> 00:01:29,035
So here's evening one, with everything taken out that belongs only to evening one.

21
00:01:30,835 --> 00:01:33,009
A landmark, and three steps.

22
00:01:33,909 --> 00:01:36,952
The words in capitals are the slots.

23
00:01:38,752 --> 00:01:40,926
The evening's number, two digits.

24
00:01:41,826 --> 00:01:46,609
The system, twice, and it must be the same both times.

25
00:01:47,509 --> 00:01:49,248
What's in the way.

26
00:01:50,148 --> 00:01:51,887
What finishes the objective.

27
00:01:52,337 --> 00:01:54,076
And what it pays.

28
00:01:55,876 --> 00:01:59,354
The open ends with the count so far,

29
00:01:59,804 --> 00:02:03,283
a table with one row for each boat.

30
00:02:04,183 --> 00:02:07,226
You copy it from the open before,

31
00:02:07,676 --> 00:02:09,415
and add a row.

32
00:02:11,215 --> 00:02:12,954
Everything else is yours:

33
00:02:13,404 --> 00:02:16,883
four titles, and four short pieces of text.

34
00:02:18,683 --> 00:02:21,726
Keep the pattern in your campaign page,

35
00:02:22,176 --> 00:02:25,654
so it's there every time you sit down.

36
00:02:26,854 --> 00:02:32,507
The slot that changes the evening most is the ending of the objective.

37
00:02:34,307 --> 00:02:38,220
Scan, and it's an evening about finding something out.

38
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:43,467
Destroy, and it's a fight they can't walk away from.

39
00:02:45,267 --> 00:02:47,441
Signal, and it's a conversation,

40
00:02:47,891 --> 00:02:50,935
which we'll write in a later lecture.

41
00:02:51,835 --> 00:02:53,574
And then there's reach,

42
00:02:54,024 --> 00:02:57,937
for an evening that's about getting somewhere further on.

43
00:02:59,737 --> 00:03:03,215
One of those four goes in the slot,

44
00:03:03,665 --> 00:03:08,013
and the rest of the pattern doesn't change at all.

45
00:03:09,213 --> 00:03:11,387
The sheet first, as always.

46
00:03:12,287 --> 00:03:14,461
Then the landmark, the Dunlin,

47
00:03:14,911 --> 00:03:16,650
in cloud, and guarded.

48
00:03:18,450 --> 00:03:20,624
Now look at the open.

49
00:03:21,074 --> 00:03:23,683
I don't have to write it.

50
00:03:24,583 --> 00:03:28,496
It's already there, because it was last week's hook.

51
00:03:28,946 --> 00:03:31,554
It only needs its next step.

52
00:03:33,354 --> 00:03:35,528
So I write three things.

53
00:03:36,428 --> 00:03:39,037
The objective: this one's a trap,

54
00:03:39,487 --> 00:03:42,096
and they have to break it.

55
00:03:42,996 --> 00:03:46,909
Its text starts with a ship, a Torgoth hull,

56
00:03:47,359 --> 00:03:49,533
because the Gleaners fly Torgoth.

57
00:03:50,433 --> 00:03:53,911
The crew sees what's waiting before they jump.

58
00:03:55,711 --> 00:03:57,450
Then the way home,

59
00:03:57,900 --> 00:04:00,509
and the hook for evening three.

60
00:04:01,409 --> 00:04:03,583
The hook copies the count,

61
00:04:04,033 --> 00:04:07,076
and adds a row for the Plover.

62
00:04:08,876 --> 00:04:11,920
That's the rhythm of the whole campaign.

63
00:04:12,820 --> 00:04:16,733
Every time you sit down, the open's already written,

64
00:04:17,183 --> 00:04:23,270
and you write a place, a deed, a way home, and the next hook.

65
00:04:24,470 --> 00:04:26,643
Two details in that objective.

66
00:04:28,443 --> 00:04:30,617
The game sends three guards,

67
00:04:31,067 --> 00:04:33,241
and I ask for two.

68
00:04:34,141 --> 00:04:37,185
A fight to the very last ship

69
00:04:37,635 --> 00:04:41,548
goes on five minutes after it stopped being fun.

70
00:04:43,348 --> 00:04:46,391
And the fight needs the guards line.

71
00:04:47,291 --> 00:04:49,030
I measured it without:

72
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:52,524
there was nothing hostile there at all,

73
00:04:52,974 --> 00:04:55,583
and the evening couldn't be finished.

74
00:04:57,383 --> 00:05:00,426
So an objective that ends with destroy

75
00:05:00,876 --> 00:05:04,354
always gets a guards line on its landmark.

76
00:05:05,554 --> 00:05:07,293
Lint says it's clean.

77
00:05:08,193 --> 00:05:12,107
So I play evening one through to its hook,

78
00:05:12,557 --> 00:05:15,165
and I engage the new lead.

79
00:05:16,965 --> 00:05:20,878
Here's the Dunlin, the warning, and the new step.

80
00:05:21,778 --> 00:05:24,822
One ship down, and it's still open.

81
00:05:25,272 --> 00:05:27,011
Two, and it's done.

82
00:05:28,811 --> 00:05:30,985
Then we're home and paid,

83
00:05:31,435 --> 00:05:34,478
and there's the lead for evening three.

84
00:05:36,278 --> 00:05:40,626
And the charted places now have both boats in them.

85
00:05:41,826 --> 00:05:47,478
So the open of every evening is the hook of the one before.

86
00:05:48,378 --> 00:05:50,552
You never start from nothing.

87
00:05:52,352 --> 00:05:55,830
Renumber every key when you copy the pattern.

88
00:05:56,730 --> 00:06:01,948
If you don't, the game keeps the first record with that key,

89
00:06:02,398 --> 00:06:05,007
and quietly drops your new evening.

90
00:06:05,907 --> 00:06:08,515
Lint does tell you, by name.

91
00:06:10,315 --> 00:06:12,924
And the pattern's yours to break.

92
00:06:13,374 --> 00:06:15,548
A tentpole can be longer.

93
00:06:16,448 --> 00:06:19,926
Next time, all twenty evenings, on one page.
